{"availability":"available","full":"nitsuah/kryptos","name":"kryptos","description":"Research toolkit for systematically attacking the unsolved Kryptos K4 cipher using classical cryptanalysis, layered hypotheses, automated experiments, and reproducible validation.","category":"Developer tools","language":"Python","stars":1,"forks":0,"created":"2025-05-26T14:20:03Z","updated":"2026-10-19T09:10:17Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://kryptos-k4.netlify.app","topics":[],"demo":"https://kryptos-k4.netlify.app/","color":"#63362a","ink":"#f8c7a8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-19T18:04:42.479Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-19T18:04:42.479Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-19T18:04:42.479Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-10T11:07:49.750Z","stars":1,"forks":0},{"at":"2026-10-16T07:13:12.821Z","stars":1,"forks":0},{"at":"2026-10-19T18:04:42.479Z","stars":1,"forks":0}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["🧭 kryptos · Index · Features · Roadmap · Tasks · Changelog · Metrics","Inspired by The Unexplained with William Shatner, I set out to solve Kryptos using Python.","Kryptos is the copper sculpture Jim Sanborn installed at CIA headquarters in 1990. Three of its four passages (K1–K3) were solved in the 1990s. The last 97 letters, K4, are still unsolved. Sanborn has published 24 of its plaintext letters as hints: EAST, NORTHEAST, BERLIN and CLOCK."],"features":["Eliminates cipher families. For each family (periodic keys, autokey, Hill, columnar transposition plus a key,","Tracks what is left. A hypothesis ledger tags every family as eliminated, statistical, samplednull or"],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/nitsuah/kryptos/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"42067a6703a35ce63c329f1f5f601a596abc7aaaa5969f96b2576c4dc42b9fd4","extractedAt":"2026-10-10T11:07:49.851Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-19T18:04:42.604Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"🧭 kryptos · Index · Features · Roadmap · Tasks · Changelog · Metrics","sourceUr